## INTRODUCTION
Multifunctional spintronic devices that respond to electrical, magnetic, and chemical stimuli have drawn much attention in recent years due to their important role in downsizing logic circuits.1–3 One class of materials that show promise towards such applications are two-dimensional (2D) electrically conductive metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) made from paramagnetic ions that in principle respond to electrical, magnetic, as well as chemical stimuli.4– 17 These 2D MOFs have layered graphene-like honeycomb structures, where the vertices in the hexagonal tiles are the tritopic organic ligands and the transition metals ions bridging the ligands reside on the edges of each hexagon (Figure 1).4,10,17 Because the ligands and metals in MOFs can be independently modified or chosen, these materials present opportunities that are not easily achieved with graphite or other 2D materials, allowing for control over porosity, conductivity, and spin state. However, the
extended structures and compositional complexity also translate into complicated electronic structures that give rise to electrical and magnetic properties that are still poorly understood despite numerous experimental and computational studies.8,9,18–20 One powerful means to interrogate the properties of solids has been dimensional reduction, wherein the independent synthesis and study of smaller building blocks provide insight into the electronic structure of the parent solids.21,22 The 2D conductive materials described above make excellent targets for this strategy. Here, we report trinuclear Cu complexes bridged by the same trigonal ligands found in the MOFs and study their redox and magnetic properties.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
In a manner mimicking the isolation of small clusters related to Prussian blue analogs,23 we employed ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ complexes capped with the neutral multidentate $\kappa_{3}$ -chelating ligand 1,4,7-trimethyl- $^{\cdot1,4,7}$ -triazacyclononane $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacn}\right)$ to target the MOF model complexes. Importantly, the typical coordination mode of $\bf{M e}_{3}t a c n$ keeps the copper magnetic orbital $\big(\mathrm{d}_{\mathbf{x}^{2}-\mathbf{y}^{2}}\big)$ in the aromatic plane of the ligand, as is also likely the case in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HXTP}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(X\=\O}$ , I). Because in the extended MOFs the ligand is formally triply oxidized – and thus bears a formal oxidation state of $^{-3}$ after six-fold deprotonation of the amino or hydroxy groups – we targeted the same oxidation state in the molecular complexes (Scheme 1, left). In view of the expected rich redox reactivity of $[\mathrm{M}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}]^{n+}$ complexes, $24^{-26}$ the target compounds $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}(\mathrm{HXTP})]^{n+}$ were synthesized under inert atmosphere followed by stoichiometric oxidations with three equivalent of oxidants. Thus, reaction of $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacn})\mathrm{Cu}](\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}$ with $^{2,3,6,7,10,11-}$ hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP) under $\Nu_{2}$ , followed by deprotonation and oxidation with three equivalents of ferrocenium tetrafluoroborate $(\mathrm{FcBF_{4}})$ yielded $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacn}\mathrm{Cu})_{3}(\mathrm{HOTP)](\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{3}\left(\mathbf{n}\cdot(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{3}\right)}$ as dark navy crystals in $73\%$ yield (Scheme S1). Unexpectedly, a similar procedure utilizing 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaaminotriphenylene (HATP) instead of HHTP did not lead to the isolation of the desired $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})]^{3+}$ complex. Instead, upon reaction of HATP with $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacn})\mathrm{Cu}]^{2+}$ and oxidation by three equivalents of $\mathrm{FcBF}_{4}$ , an initially dark blue-green solution changes to a dark blue solution whereupon only $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacn}\mathrm{Cu})_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})](\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{4}({\bf2\cdot}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{4})$ could be isolated (Scheme 1, right; also Scheme S1). This more electrondeficient species containing the quadruply oxidized ligand ${\mathrm{HITP^{2-}}}$ suggests that $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})]^{3+}$ is unstable with respect to disproportionation, a redox behavior that diverges from that of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ under otherwise similar synthetic conditions. The identities of $\textbf{1}$ and 2 were confirmed by high-resolution electrospray ionization/mass spectrometry (ESI/MS) and microelemental analyses. The former gave $\mathrm{m/z}$ values of $1285{\cdot}3$ for 1 $\scriptstyle([\mathsf{M}+\mathsf{H}]^{+}$ , $\textbf{M}=$ $[(\mathrm{C_{9}H_{21}N_{3}C u)_{3}(C_{18}H_{6}O_{6})](B F_{4})_{3}})$ and $\mathrm{m/z}=1365{\cdot}4$ for $\scriptstyle2$ $\mathrm{([M\mathrm{+H}]^{+}}$ , $\begin{array}{r l r}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}}&{=}&{[(\ensuremath{\mathrm{C}}_{9}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{21}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}_{3}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cu}})_{3}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{C}}_{18}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{12}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}_{6})](\ensuremath{\mathrm{BF}}_{4})_{4},}\end{array}$ see also Supporting Information).
Single crystals of 1 and 2 suitable for X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis were obtained by layering or diffusing vapors of $t$ -butyl-methyl-ether to solutions of the respective complexes in $\mathbf{4:1}$ mixtures of dichloroethane:methanol at $-5$ $^{\circ}C$ . Compounds 1 and $\scriptstyle2$ crystallize in space groups $\mathrm{\sfP}\bar{1}$ and $\mathrm{P}_{2_{1}}/\mathrm{c}$ , respectively (Fig. 2a, c). In the solid state, both 1 and 2 pack in dimeric fashion, with closest C…C distances of $_{3.086(5)}$ and 3.223(6) Å between the dimers (Fig. 2b, d; right). A top-down view of the dimers reveals staggered conformations for both complexes (Fig. 2b,d). In both 1 and 2, the longest $\mathrm{Cu}{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacn}}}$ bonds are the axial ones, as expected due to the Jahn-Teller distortion, and confirming the co-planarity of the $\operatorname{Cu}\mathrm{d}_{\mathbf{x}^{2}-\mathbf{y}^{2}}$ magnetic orbital with the HXTP plane. For metal-semiquinone complexes, the C–O bond length is often related to the degree of oxidation of the semiquinone fragment. Shorter C–O bonds indicate more oxidized (quinone-type) character whereas longer C–O bonds correspond to reduced (catechol-type) character.27 For 1, the average C–O bond length is $1.283(\stackrel{.}{4})\stackrel{.}{\mathrm{A}}.$ , in very good agreement with the value of 1.288(2) $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ reported for an established copper bis-semiquinonate complex.28 In contrast, 2 exhibits an average C–N bond length of 1.305(6) Å, shorter than that of a structurally relevant bisiminosemiquinonate copper azophenine complex $\left(1.327(3)\right.$ Å).29 Although subtle, the difference between the shorter average C–N bond length in 2 relative to the established iminosemiquinonate Cu complex is consistent with a higher degree of oxidation and partial iminoquinone character in 2.
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) provided further support for assigning formal oxidation states in 1 and 2 and offered clues into the degree of electron delocalization in the trinuclear complexes. CV experiments for complex 1 conducted in 0.1 M propylene carbonate solutions of tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate $\mathrm{(TBAPF_{6})}$ ) under $\Nu_{2}$ reveal three reversible reduction events at ‒0.80 V, $-0.45\mathrm{V}$ , ‒0.09 V relative to the ferrocenium/ferrocene $\mathrm{(Fc^{+}/F c)}$ couple (Fig. 3a). All three values are in line with expected ligandcentered redox couples: ${\mathrm{HOTP}}^{3-/4-}$ , $\mathrm{HOTP^{4-/5^{-}}}$ , and HOTP5‒ $^{/6-},$ , the last corresponding to the formation of neutral $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}(\mathrm{HOTP})].$ 25,30 In the framework of the classical theory of mixed-valence compounds, which relates electrochemical redox potentials to the degree of electronic delocalization between mixed-valence centers,31,32 the potential difference between the ${\mathrm{HOTP5^{-/6-}}}$ and ${\mathrm{HOTP}}^{3-/4^{-}}$ couples, $\Delta\mathrm{~E~}=\mathrm{~o.71~V~}$ , equates to a comproportionation constant,33 $\mathrm{K_{c}}$ , of $\mathbf{10^{12.0}}$ . This large value indicates that the three redox centers – the three semiquinone/hydroquinone moieties of each HOTP ligand – are fully delocalized and form a Robin-Day class III mixed-valent compound,34 as may be expected for three formal radicals, all centered on a single organic moiety. A similar analysis for $\scriptstyle2$ is made difficult by the irreversibility of the first reduction event, presumably the $\mathrm{HITP^{2-/3^{-}}}$ couple, which occurs at –0.51 V (Fig. 3b). The irreversible reduction of $\scriptstyle2$ is in line with rapid disproportionation of the $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})]^{3+}$ species, which further prevents access to more reduced complexes. Although the strong degree of charge delocalization within HOTP is in-line with the extended delocalization observed in the respective HOTP-based MOF, inaccessible $\mathtt{C u}$ -based redox events with either 1 or 2 prevent the assessment of charge delocalization between the metal centers, as mediated by HOTP or HITP.
Magnetometry provided critical information about the electronic communication between metals and/or ligands in complexes 1 and 2. Variable temperature direct current magnetic susceptibility measurements indicated antiferromagnetic coupling for both complexes, as revealed by rapid decreases of $\chi_{M}T$ below $50~\mathrm{K}$ in an applied field of $2.5\mathrm{kOe}$ (Fig. 4a). For 1, a room temperature $\chi_{M}T$ value of 1.79 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mol^{-1}K}$ is close to the value expected for four independent $S\ =\ ^{1}/2$ spins (1.50 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mol^{-1}K}$ , with $g\ =\ 2.0)$ , representing three non-interacting ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and one $S=\%$ HOTP3‒ organic radical. Because of a significant contribution from temperature independent paramagnetism (TIP) (see Fig. $S_{2}$ and related discussion for assignment of TIP), the room-temperature $\chi_{M}T$ value for $\scriptstyle2$ is $2.32\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}$ , higher than expected for three uncoupled ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions connected through the diamagnetic ${\mathrm{HITP^{2-}}}$ (see Fig. S3 for $\chi_{M}T$ vs. T plot where the contributions of TIP were corrected for). The plateau $\chi_{M}T$ value for $\scriptstyle2$ of 0.42 $:{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{mol}}^{-1}{\mathrm{K}}$ at $_{1.8\mathrm{~K~}}$ is nevertheless close to the value expected for a $S=$ $_{1/2}$ spin system $(\mathrm{0.375cm^{3}m o l^{-1}K}$ with $\bf\delta g=2.0$ ). This suggests that the ground spin state of $\scriptstyle2$ is $S=1/2$ , as would be expected for three antiferromagnetically coupled ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions at low temperature.
To assess the magnitude of the spin exchange interactions, the temperature-dependent $\chi_{M}T$ data were fitted with Heisenberg-Dirac-Van Vleck (HDVV) Hamiltonians, $\mathbf{H_{1}}$ for 1 and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ for $\scriptstyle2$ (see also Fig. S4):
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathbf{H}_{1}=-2J_{1}(\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu1}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{L}}+\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu2}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{L}})-2J_{2}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu3}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{L}}}\\ &{\mathbf{H}_{2}=-2J_{1}(\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu1}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu2}}+\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu1}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu3}})-2J_{2}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu2}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{Cu3}}}\end{array}
$$
Good fits were obtained for both complexes by using two $J$ values representing only the spin interactions between nearest neighbors. For 1, fitting with contribution from TIP and intermolecular coupling, $z J^{\prime}$ , as well as two $g$ values for copper- and ligand-centered spins gives $J_{1}=J_{2}=-2.76~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (with $g_{\mathrm{Cu}}=2.06$ , $g_{\mathrm{L}}=1.98$ , $\chi_{\mathrm{TIP}}=8.86{\cdot}10^{-4}~\mathrm{cm^{3}\ m o l^{-1}}$ , $z J=-$ $\mathbf{o.309~cm^{-1}},$ ) (Fig. 4a) (see Fig. $S_{5}$ and related discussion on the choice of $g$ ). This indicates that the ligand-based radical is coupled to the three ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions through weak antiferromagnetic interactions of similar strength. As such, the ground spin state of 1 is $S={\bf1}$ , with the trigonal symmetry preserved in the spin structure despite a geometric distortion from perfect planarity. For 2, the best fit of the temperature-dependent $\chi_{M}T$ data gave $J_{1}=J_{2}=-6.99~\mathrm{cm^{-1}};$ with $g_{\mathrm{Cu}}=2.12$ and $\chi_{T I P}=3{\cdot}7^{1{\cdot}10^{-3}}\mathrm{\cm^{3}m o l^{-1}}$ (Fig. 4a). To assess the possibility that paramagnetic impurities contribute to the magnetic properties of 1 and $^2$ , we fitted the data by adding $\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{\textperthousand~}$ impurities. This produced fits with similar parameters as for the pure samples above, with negligible contribution from the $S=\%$ impurities (Fig. S6 and related discussions). The lack of crystalline impurities is also supported by the powder X-ray diffraction analysis for 1 and $\scriptstyle2$ (see Fig. $S_{7}$ and related discussions).
The ground spin states of complexes 1 and 2 were confirmed by variable field magnetization measurements (Fig. 4b). For 1, the magnetization does not saturate even at 90 kOe and continues to grow after reaching an inflection point of $\mathbf{\tilde{\Pi}}^{\mathbf{\tilde{\Pi}}}\mathbf{\tilde{\Pi}}^{\mathbf{\tilde{\Pi}}}$ B.M. at approximately 40 $\mathbf{kOe}$ , a behavior that is typically associated with the presence of low-lying excited spin states.35 The magnetization curve for 1 further deviates from typical Brillouin behavior, likely because of additional intermolecular antiferromagnetic interactions that lower the expected saturation value of 2 B.M. for an $S={\bf1}$ system (see Fig. S8 and related discussion in the Supporting Information). In contrast, the magnetization of complex 2 follows typical Brillouin-type behavior and shows a saturation value of 1 B.M., as expected for an $S=1/2$ system. Fitting of this data with a Brillouin function gives $g=2.0$ , a slightly smaller value than usually observed for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , again likely owing to weak intermolecular antiferromagnetic interactions. Low temperature electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) provided further insight on the electronic structure of 1 and $\scriptstyle2$ . For 1, an isotropic $S=1/2$ signal at $g=$ 2.00 and an axial ${\cal S}={\bf1}/2$ signal with $g_{\parallel}=2.25$ , $g_{\perp}=2.05$ , and hyperfine coupling to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ $\left(I=3/2\right)$ with $A_{\parallel}\sim30\mathrm{~G~}$ were observed (Fig. S9). This agrees with the observation of single unpaired spins on both HOTP and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ from magnetometry measurements in 1. For $\scriptstyle2$ , only an axial ${\cal S}=\mathbf{1}/2$ signal with $g_{\parallel}=2.25\$ , $g_{\perp}=2.05$ is observed, which gives a good fit with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ hyperfine coupling constants of $A_{\parallel}=40\mathrm{~G~}$ and $A_{\perp}=413$ G, suggesting the presence of $S=1/2$ spins coupled to $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ in 2. This is in good agreement with the magnetometry data that suggests the absence of an HITP-based radical in 2.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations carried out for the cationic fragments $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}]^{3+}$ and $[(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{tacnCu})_{3}\mathrm{HITP}]4+$ corroborate magnetometry data and provide further insight into the orbital interactions involved in the spin exchange mechanisms in 1 and 2. For both complexes, DFT calculations agree with the proposed spin density distribution derived from magnetometry data above. The magnetic orbitals of the copper centers have the same symmetry as the $\mathbf{d}_{\mathbf{x}^{2}-\mathbf{y}^{2}}$ atomic orbitals, and lie in the plane of the molecule (Fig. 5). For both 1 and $\_$ , the oxygen and nitrogen atoms of the HXTP $\mathrm{(X=O,I,}$ ) ligands have primarily the same sign of spin density as the copper atoms, suggesting ferromagnetic exchange due to orbital orthogonality. Meanwhile, the dominating antiferromagnetic interaction in both complexes is embodied through spin polarization or antiferromagnetic superexchange mechanisms in the p-π systems of the ligands.
Altogether, the structural, electrochemical, and magnetic data support a ligand-based $S~=~\%$ radical in 1, which therefore represents a first example of a molecular complex modeling electronic interactions in a conductive MOF, in this case $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}_{2}$ . Notably, even though other examples of  isolable $\mathrm{M}_{3}\mathrm{HXTP}$ -type complexes $\mathrm{(X=O,N,}S$ ) exist, none of these show spin density on both the metal and the ligand. $^{24-26,30,32,36}$ Although 1 is a truncated model of an idealized single-sheet of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}_{2}$ , which to our knowledge has not been accessed yet, if the antiferromagnetic interactions observed in 1 are reproduced in a single sheet of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}_{2}$ , the latter may exhibit properties consistent with a conductive 2D ferrimagnet.20,37 Verifying such rare behavior rests entirely with efforts to generate and exfoliate sufficiently large single crystals of $\mathrm{Cu_{3}H O T P_{2}}$ , an area of ongoing interest in our laboratory.
In the same vein, it should be noted that 2 satisfies the classical geometric spin frustration criterion of $\stackrel{\cdot}{J_{1}}=J_{2}<0$ in a spin triangle, as proposed by Kahn.38 Although it has been established that true geometric spin frustration cannot be achieved in molecular complexes due to fast geometric relaxation,39,40 the type of interactions observed in 2 bring about the possibility of a geometrically frustrated solid state material, where structural relaxation to lower symmetry is quenched by lattice rigidity. As with 1, if it exists, spin frustration is likely to be observed only in single, charged sheets of extended MOFs of the type $\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ with no inter-sheet interactions.41,42 Once again, the challenge in accessing such 2D structures rests squarely in the synthetic realm and highlights the importance of controlling crystal growth for 2D conductive MOFs.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we present the synthesis, electrochemical, and magnetic characterization of two new trinuclear copper complexes that mimic the smallest building blocks of conductive 2D MOFs $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ . Whereas the oxygen-based complex retains a ligand-based radical similar to the corresponding $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HOTP}_{2}$ , the nitrogen-based ligand radical disproportionates, thereby diverging from the formal oxidation state present in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ . In both complexes, the metal and/or ligand-based electronic spins are coupled through weak antiferromagnetic interactions. These results portend potentially exciting long-range magnetic interactions in isolated single sheets of the respective 2D MOFs, substantiating the importance of future efforts to isolate such sheets.
